# OPA/Rego Policy Standards

## When to Use OPA/Rego
Use OPA policies for **plan-time governance** evaluated against `terraform show -json` output:
- Enforce organizational policies across all modules from one place
- Evaluate complex cross-resource relationships
- Gate plan approval in CI/CD pipelines
- Policy logic that needs version-controlled, reviewable rules

## Policy Layout

```
policies/
  {{COMPANY_SLUG}}/
    naming.rego                ← naming convention checks
    naming_test.rego
    tags.rego                  ← required tag checks
    tags_test.rego
    network.rego               ← network hardening checks
    network_test.rego
  data/
    allowed_regions.json       ← reference data files
    required_tags.json
```

## Policy Boilerplate

```rego
package {{COMPANY_SLUG}}.{{POLICY_AREA}}

import rego.v1

# Collect all violations — callers check `count(violations) == 0`
violations contains msg if {
    some resource in input.resource_changes
    resource.type == "{{TERRAFORM_RESOURCE_TYPE}}"
    resource.change.actions[_] in {"create", "update"}
    {{VIOLATION_CONDITION}}
    msg := sprintf("{{ERROR_MESSAGE_TEMPLATE}}", [{{MSG_ARGS}}])
}
```
<!-- Example violation condition for tag check:
    not resource.change.after.tags["environment"]
    msg := sprintf("Resource %s is missing required tag 'environment'", [resource.address])
-->

## Evaluating Policies Against a Plan

```bash
# Generate plan JSON
terraform plan -out=tfplan.binary
terraform show -json tfplan.binary > tfplan.json

# Evaluate with OPA
opa eval \
  --input tfplan.json \
  --data policies/ \
  --format pretty \
  "data.{{COMPANY_SLUG}}.{{POLICY_AREA}}.violations"

# Non-zero exit if any violations
opa eval \
  --input tfplan.json \
  --data policies/ \
  --fail-defined \
  "data.{{COMPANY_SLUG}}.{{POLICY_AREA}}.violations"
```

## Policy Test Boilerplate

```rego
package {{COMPANY_SLUG}}.{{POLICY_AREA}}_test

import rego.v1

# --- passing case ---
test_no_violation_when_compliant if {
    count(violations) == 0 with input as {
        "resource_changes": [{
            "address": "{{TERRAFORM_RESOURCE_TYPE}}.default",
            "type": "{{TERRAFORM_RESOURCE_TYPE}}",
            "change": {
                "actions": ["create"],
                "after": {
                    {{COMPLIANT_RESOURCE_ATTRIBUTES}}
                }
            }
        }]
    }
}

# --- failing case ---
test_violation_when_non_compliant if {
    count(violations) > 0 with input as {
        "resource_changes": [{
            "address": "{{TERRAFORM_RESOURCE_TYPE}}.default",
            "type": "{{TERRAFORM_RESOURCE_TYPE}}",
            "change": {
                "actions": ["create"],
                "after": {
                    {{NON_COMPLIANT_RESOURCE_ATTRIBUTES}}
                }
            }
        }]
    }
}
```

## Running Policy Tests
```bash
# Run all policy tests
opa test policies/ -v

# Run tests for a specific package
opa test policies/{{COMPANY_SLUG}}/ -v --run {{POLICY_AREA}}
```

## CI Integration Pattern
```yaml
- name: Evaluate OPA policies
  run: |
    terraform show -json tfplan.binary > tfplan.json
    opa eval \
      --input tfplan.json \
      --data policies/ \
      --fail-defined \
      "data.{{COMPANY_SLUG}}.main.violations"
```

## Conventions
- One package per policy area (`naming`, `tags`, `network`, `iam`)
- Always expose violations as a **set named `violations`** for consistent CI queries
- Test files co-located with policy files, suffix `_test.rego`
- Reference data (allowed regions, required tag keys) in `data/` as JSON — never hardcode in Rego
